--- !u!114 &1774518185476321660
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 7549029159763388547}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3ff6acaa8b9f1994499dd387e6188532, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  Comments: 'This demonstrates stereoscopic rendering (left and right eye) using
    2 cameras. Every frame, both cameras are rendered outputting either to top-bottom
    or left-right layout, or saving left and right images in separate directories.
    This mode does not apply any camera distortion nor does it render cubemaps (not
    360 or VR, but stereoscopic only).


    Interocular Distance

    For use in
    production the interocular distance (between cameras) and other settings may
    need to be adjusted to your project. The interocular distance is set by slightly
    shifting the camera positions. This is to simulate the distance between human
    eyes for a natural feel. Typically this is a value between 50 and 75mm. For this
    example, 60mm is used which provides an interocular distance in meters of 0.06.
    This is applied to the left camera only to leave the right camera centered, since
    most people are right-eye dominant. This allows the right side to be used for
    standard flatscreen video and maintain centering for titles.'
  Warning: Certain rendering features such as screen space effects, some shaders,
    and post processing effects may not be supported in a stereo rendering setup.
    This can result in rendering anomalies between left and right eye. To avoid these
    issues, test early on and decide on rendering features that do work with multi-eye
    rendering. Also refer to Unity's documentation for single and multipass rendering.
  URLTitle: Render To Disk Documentation
  URL: https://axongenesis.gitbook.io/timeflow/reference/behaviors/rendering/render-to-disk
  URL2Title: Stereo Rendering
  URL2: https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/SinglePassStereoRendering.html
